// `io.flush()` — push what has been written so far, whole line or not. A live host streams
// completed lines, so a fragment with no newline waits for whatever terminates it; `flush` is the
// door for output that must arrive before that, like a prompt or a progress indicator.
//
// What this case pins is the half the deterministic sandbox can see, and it is the half most
// easily broken: a host that batch-captures output (the oracle, `noeta test`, an embedder) must
// come out of a flushing program byte-identical to a non-flushing one. `flush` offers the buffers
// to the host and takes them back untouched when the host declines, so fragments still join into
// the lines below and stdout and stderr stay separate. A flush that dropped what the host did not
// take would show up here as missing output.
// expect: stdout "start"
// expect: stdout "partial then flushed then more"
// expect: stdout "42"
// expect: stdout "end"
// expect: stderr "warn: half flushed, half not"
// expect: exit 0
use std.io

echo "start"

// A fragment, flushed mid-line: the line is still one line when it lands.
io.out("partial")
io.flush()
io.out(" then flushed")
io.flush()
io.outln(" then more")

// Flushing an empty buffer is fine, and flushing twice in a row is the same as flushing once.
io.flush()
io.flush()

// Values render the way `echo` renders them, flush or no flush.
io.out(42)
io.flush()
io.outln("")

// stderr flushes with stdout and keeps its own buffer.
io.err("warn: half flushed")
io.flush()
io.errln(", half not")

echo "end"
